{"candidates":[{"file":"src/users/users.service.ts","symbol":"UsersService.create","start_line":29,"end_line":129,"explanation":"I couldn't see the code, so this is inferred from the symptom and the retrieved structure. `AuthService.register` doesn't set a provider. `UsersService.create` fills it in, and the default should be `AuthProvidersEnum.email`. The login error means the saved user has `provider = 'google'`, so I suspect this default is wrong. It could be using `AuthProvidersEnum.google`, or taking a provider from the DTO that isn't email."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.register","start_line":200,"end_line":232,"explanation":"This builds the `create` payload for a self-registered user. If it spreads the DTO or passes a provider other than `AuthProvidersEnum.email`, the new user is saved with the wrong provider. `validateLogin` would then reject the email login with `needLoginViaProvider:google`."},{"file":"src/auth/auth.service.ts","symbol":"AuthService.validateLogin","start_line":42,"end_line":97,"explanation":"This is the check that returns 422 `needLoginViaProvider:${user.provider}` when `user.provider !== AuthProvidersEnum.email`. It's less likely to be the cause, because its output is a symptom of the stored provider. I'd only look here if the comparison itself is wrong, for example against the wrong enum member."}],"fix":{"file":"src/users/users.service.ts","description":"In `UsersService.create`, set the provider to `createUserDto.provider ?? AuthProvidersEnum.email`. Make sure `AuthService.register` doesn't pass or override the provider with anything other than `AuthProvidersEnum.email`."}}
